/*
RMSTEST_41a - elevation bias vs CLUMP DENSITY, 250 clumps
preview-design.md Sec.15 item 11 (Sec.6.2's model)

READ THIS ON WHICHEVER OF 41a/41b/41c YOU OPEN FIRST - one experiment, and only
the clump count differs. Per-clump budget is pinned at the measured 6-tile floor
in all three.

TWO HYPOTHESES HAVE ALREADY DIED HERE, SO READ THE DESIGN BEFORE THE RESULT
  - "growth is biased": RMSTEST_40a/40b hold the clump-size ratio FLAT at 1.16
    and 1.13 across a 4x change in per-clump budget. Growth is not meaningfully
    biased. (40c could not be read - 36.5 components from a declared 50, so its
    sizes are merged-component sizes.)
  - "the ratio tracks coverage": 40b sits at 3.29% coverage and gives 1.41,
    while RMSTEST_39 at 1.55% gives 1.88 and RMSTEST_35 at 6.21% gives 7.16.
    Not monotone. Coverage is not the driver.

What survives is CLUMP DENSITY - clumps per tile - which is the only single
variable that orders all six runs held so far (0.00125 -> ~1.4-2.1, 0.0025 ->
1.88, 0.0125 -> 7.16, 0.0347 -> 12.86). Note the last two are the SAME script at
two map sizes, so the "map size" effect chased earlier was clump density.

There is no mechanism for it. This trio is the test.

WHY THE TILE RATIO IS THE READING AND MERGING NO LONGER MATTERS
At high clump counts components merge and the seed/size decomposition degrades -
that is what spoiled RMSTEST_35 and 40c. **The TILE ratio is immune: merging
changes which tiles group together, never which tiles are elevated.** So unlike
every previous attempt this design stays readable at every point in the sweep.
Report the decomposition too, but read the tile ratio.

THE DESIGN'S REAL STRENGTH - MATCHED-COVERAGE PAIRS
Coverage rises with clump count here, which would normally confound the two.
It does not, because each point has a partner already measured at nearly the
same coverage and a very different clump count:

  41a  250 clumps, 3.75% cover   vs  40b   50 clumps, 3.29% cover -> 1.41
  41c 1000 clumps, 15.0% cover   vs  40c   50 clumps, 13.1% cover -> 2.08

Matched coverage, 5x and 20x the clumps. If density drives the bias, 41a and 41c
come back far above their partners. If they land near 1.4-2.1, density is dead
too and the high ratios in RMSTEST_35/22a come from something else again.

  41b  500 clumps at 6 tiles     vs  RMSTEST_35  500 clumps at 4 tiles -> 7.16
Same clump count, different per-clump budget. If 41b reproduces ~7, clump count
is confirmed and the budget is irrelevant; if 41b comes back near 1.5, then the
sub-floor 4-tile budget in RMSTEST_35 is implicated and the whole density story
is an artefact of that one script.

READ IT WITH
  Connected components of elevation > 0.
  Per run: component count against the declared 250; centroid favoured:disfavoured
  split; mean component size per side; and the raw TILE ratio, which is the
  reading. Report coverage so the pairing above can be checked rather than assumed.

PREDICTIONS, RECORDED BEFORE THE RUN
1. DENSITY HOLDS: 41a well above 1.41 (interpolating, ~3-4), 41b near 7, 41c
   highest. Then Sec.6.2 gets a density-indexed amplification, fitted and marked
   [tune], with the mechanism still unknown but the behaviour predictable.
2. DENSITY DIES: all three land near 1.4-2.1 like their matched partners. Then
   three hypotheses have failed, RMSTEST_35 and 22a are the odd runs rather than
   the rule, and the honest move is to stop modelling the ratio and fit the
   qualitative behaviour only.
3. 41b splits the difference from the other two - reproducing ~7 while 41a/41c
   stay low - and the driver is the sub-floor per-clump budget, not density at
   all. That would be the third mechanism this item has produced and the first
   one nobody predicted.

GENERATE AT NORMAL (200) WITH ANY PLAYER COUNT - no player lands, so the count
does not enter. RUN EACH OF THE THREE FOUR TIMES (twelve generations), and
REGENERATE BETWEEN SAVES - the check is that the elevated-tile total differs run
to run.
*/

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
  base_terrain GRASS

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>

  create_elevation 1
  {
    number_of_clumps 250
    number_of_tiles 1500
    spacing 1
  }

/* NO <CLIFF_GENERATION> HEADER - an empty one generates cliffs. */

<TERRAIN_GENERATION>

<OBJECTS_GENERATION>

<CONNECTION_GENERATION>
